{"entries":[{"stem":"blas-level3-v1","path":"contracts/blas-level3-v1.yaml","description":"BLAS Level-3 operations: syrk, trmm, symm","equations":["symm","syrk","trmm"],"obligation_types":["invariant","invariant"],"properties":["syrk symmetry","trmm identity"],"references":["Dongarra et al., 'A set of level 3 basic linear algebra subprograms', 1990"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":2,"falsification_count":6,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"blas-level3-v1 BLAS Level-3 operations: syrk, trmm, symm symm C = α·A·B + β·C where A is symmetric Identity: symm(I, B, C, α, β) = α·B + β·C Equivalent to GEMM when A is stored full syrk C = α·A·Aᵀ + β·C Output is symmetric: C = Cᵀ Identity: syrk(I, n, n, α, β) = α·I + β·C trmm B = α·A·B where A is lower triangular Identity: trmm(I, B) = α·B syrk symmetry C[i,j] = C[j,i] after syrk trmm identity trmm(I, B, α) = α·B Dongarra et al., 'A set of level 3 basic linear algebra subprograms', 1990"},{"stem":"blas-trsm-v1","path":"contracts/blas-trsm-v1.yaml","description":"Triangular solve AX=B (TRSM) — cuBLAS parity","equations":["trsm"],"obligation_types":["invariant","bound"],"properties":["Backward error","Singular detection"],"references":["Golub & Van Loan, 'Matrix Computations', 4th ed., 2013"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":2,"falsification_count":2,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"blas-trsm-v1 Triangular solve AX=B (TRSM) — cuBLAS parity trsm AX = B where A is triangular (lower or upper) ||AX - B||_F / ||B||_F < n · u Singular matrix detected (zero diagonal) Backward error ||AX - B||_F / ||B||_F < n · u Singular detection returns Err(SingularMatrix) when diagonal is zero Golub & Van Loan, 'Matrix Computations', 4th ed., 2013"},{"stem":"elementwise-kernel-v1","path":"contracts/elementwise-kernel-v1.yaml","description":"AVX2 element-wise kernels with 32-wide unrolling and zero-copy allocation","equations":["add","mul_scalar","relu"],"obligation_types":["equivalence"],"properties":["SIMD and scalar element-wise ops produce identical results"],"references":["GH-383: ReLU 0.94x vs ndarray at FFN sizes","GH-387: Add 1.17x, Mul scalar 0.86x vs ndarray","Intel Intrinsics Guide: _mm256_max_ps, _mm256_add_ps, _mm256_mul_ps"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":1,"falsification_count":1,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"elementwise-kernel-v1 AVX2 element-wise kernels with 32-wide unrolling and zero-copy allocation add y_i = a_i + b_i mul_scalar y_i = x_i * s relu y_i = max(0, x_i) SIMD and scalar element-wise ops produce identical results For all valid inputs: relu_avx2(x) == relu_scalar(x), add_avx2(a,b) == add_scalar(a,b) GH-383: ReLU 0.94x vs ndarray at FFN sizes GH-387: Add 1.17x, Mul scalar 0.86x vs ndarray Intel Intrinsics Guide: _mm256_max_ps, _mm256_add_ps, _mm256_mul_ps"},{"stem":"fft-2d-v1","path":"contracts/fft-2d-v1.yaml","description":"2D FFT via row-column decomposition","equations":["fft_2d"],"obligation_types":["invariant","invariant"],"properties":["2D impulse response","Parseval energy conservation"],"references":["Cooley & Tukey, 'An algorithm for the machine calculation of complex Fourier series', 1965"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":2,"falsification_count":2,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"fft-2d-v1 2D FFT via row-column decomposition fft_2d X[k1,k2] = sum_{n1,n2} x[n1,n2] * exp(-2*pi*i*(k1*n1/N1 + k2*n2/N2)) 2D Parseval: sum|x|^2 = (1/(N1*N2)) * sum|X|^2 Separability: 2D FFT = FFT_rows then FFT_cols Impulse: FFT_2D(delta) = 1 everywhere 2D impulse response fft_2d(delta)[k1,k2] = 1 for all k1,k2 Parseval energy conservation sum|x|^2 = (1/N) * sum|X|^2 Cooley & Tukey, 'An algorithm for the machine calculation of complex Fourier series', 1965"},{"stem":"fft-3d-v1","path":"contracts/fft-3d-v1.yaml","description":"3D FFT and batched transforms","equations":["fft_3d","fft_batched"],"obligation_types":["invariant","invariant","invariant"],"properties":["3D impulse response","3D roundtrip","Parseval conservation"],"references":["Cooley & Tukey, 'An algorithm for the machine calculation of complex Fourier series', 1965"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":3,"falsification_count":5,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"fft-3d-v1 3D FFT and batched transforms fft_3d X[k1,k2,k3] = Σ_n1 Σ_n2 Σ_n3 x[n1,n2,n3] · exp(-2πi(k1·n1/N1 + k2·n2/N2 + k3·n3/N3)) Impulse: FFT3D(δ) = 1 Roundtrip: IFFT3D(FFT3D(x)) = x within tolerance Parseval: Σ|x|² = (1/N)·Σ|X|² fft_batched For each batch b: X_b = FFT(x_b) Each batch independently satisfies 1D FFT contract Impulse per batch: FFT(δ) = 1 3D impulse response FFT3D(δ_{0,0,0}) = 1 at all k 3D roundtrip ||IFFT3D(FFT3D(x)) - x||_∞ < ε Parseval conservation Σ|x|² ≈ (1/N)·Σ|X|² Cooley & Tukey, 'An algorithm for the machine calculation of complex Fourier series', 1965"},{"stem":"fft-bluestein-v1","path":"contracts/fft-bluestein-v1.yaml","description":"Bluestein chirp-z FFT for arbitrary lengths","equations":["bluestein"],"obligation_types":["invariant","invariant","invariant"],"properties":["Roundtrip accuracy","Parseval conservation","Stockham equivalence"],"references":["Bluestein, 'A linear filtering approach to the computation of discrete Fourier transform', 1970","Rabiner, Schafer & Rader, 'The Chirp z-Transform Algorithm', 1969"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":3,"falsification_count":4,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"fft-bluestein-v1 Bluestein chirp-z FFT for arbitrary lengths bluestein X[k] = chirp[k] · IFFT(FFT(a) · FFT(b))[k] Parseval: Σ|x_n|² = (1/N)·Σ|X_k|² Roundtrip: IFFT(FFT(x)) = x within tolerance Impulse response: FFT(δ) = 1 Matches Stockham for power-of-two sizes Roundtrip accuracy ||IFFT(FFT(x)) - x||_∞ < ε Parseval conservation Σ|x_n|² ≈ (1/N)·Σ|X_k|² Stockham equivalence bluestein(x, N) = stockham(x, N) for N = 2^k Bluestein, 'A linear filtering approach to the computation of discrete Fourier transform', 1970 Rabiner, Schafer & Rader, 'The Chirp z-Transform Algorithm', 1969"},{"stem":"fft-stockham-v1","path":"contracts/fft-stockham-v1.yaml","description":"GPU-accelerated FFT via Stockham/Cooley-Tukey algorithm","equations":["dft"],"obligation_types":["conservation","invariant","linearity","equivalence"],"properties":["Parseval energy conservation","Inverse roundtrip","FFT linearity","Impulse response"],"references":["Stockham, 'High-speed convolution and correlation', AFIPS 1966","Cooley & Tukey, 'An algorithm for the machine calculation of complex Fourier series', 1965","VkFFT: Efficient Vulkan/CUDA/HIP/OpenCL/Metal FFT, IEEE 2023"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":4,"falsification_count":4,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"fft-stockham-v1 GPU-accelerated FFT via Stockham/Cooley-Tukey algorithm dft X_k = Σ_{n=0}^{N-1} x_n · exp(-2πi·k·n/N) Parseval: Σ|x_n|² = (1/N)·Σ|X_k|² Linearity: DFT(αx + βy) = α·DFT(x) + β·DFT(y) Inverse: IDFT(DFT(x)) = x Parseval energy conservation |Σ|x_n|² - (1/N)·Σ|X_k|²| < ε Inverse roundtrip |IFFT(FFT(x)) - x| < ε element-wise FFT linearity |FFT(αx + βy) - α·FFT(x) - β·FFT(y)| < ε Impulse response FFT([1, 0, ..., 0]) = [1, 1, ..., 1] Stockham, 'High-speed convolution and correlation', AFIPS 1966 Cooley & Tukey, 'An algorithm for the machine calculation of complex Fourier series', 1965 VkFFT: Efficient Vulkan/CUDA/HIP/OpenCL/Metal FFT, IEEE 2023"},{"stem":"gemv-kernel-v1","path":"contracts/gemv-kernel-v1.yaml","description":"AVX2+FMA GEMV kernel for M=1 matvec bypassing BLIS packing","equations":["gemv"],"obligation_types":["equivalence"],"properties":["SIMD and scalar GEMV produce identical results"],"references":["GH-380: matvec (M=1) performance gap vs ndarray","Goto & Van de Geijn (2008). Anatomy of High-Performance Matrix Multiplication","Intel Intrinsics Guide: _mm256_fmadd_ps"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":1,"falsification_count":1,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"gemv-kernel-v1 AVX2+FMA GEMV kernel for M=1 matvec bypassing BLIS packing gemv c[j] += Σ_{k=0}^{K-1} a[k] * B[k*N + j]   for j = 0..N-1\n\nAxpy decomposition (implemented):\n  for k in 0..K:\n    c[:] += a[k] * B[k,:]\n\n4-way K-unrolled:\n  for ki in (0..K).step_by(4):\n    c[:] += a[ki]*B[ki,:] + a[ki+1]*B[ki+1,:] + a[ki+2]*B[ki+2,:] + a[ki+3]*B[ki+3,:]\n SIMD and scalar GEMV produce identical results For all valid inputs: gemv_avx2(a, B, c) == gemv_scalar(a, B, c) GH-380: matvec (M=1) performance gap vs ndarray Goto & Van de Geijn (2008). Anatomy of High-Performance Matrix Multiplication Intel Intrinsics Guide: _mm256_fmadd_ps"},{"stem":"image-canny-v1","path":"contracts/image-canny-v1.yaml","description":"Canny edge detection (Gaussian + Sobel + NMS + hysteresis)","equations":["canny","sobel"],"obligation_types":["invariant","invariant","invariant"],"properties":["Binary output","Constant image produces no edges","Invalid thresholds rejected"],"references":["Canny, 'A Computational Approach to Edge Detection', IEEE TPAMI, 1986"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":3,"falsification_count":3,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"image-canny-v1 Canny edge detection (Gaussian + Sobel + NMS + hysteresis) canny edges = hysteresis(NMS(|grad(G_sigma * I)|)) Output is binary: all values in {0, 1} No edges on constant image Edges detected at sharp boundaries Threshold ordering: 0 <= low <= high <= 1 sobel G_x = S_x * I, G_y = S_y * I (3x3 Sobel kernels) Gradient of constant image is zero Gradient detects horizontal/vertical edges Binary output all(v in {0.0, 1.0} for v in canny(I)) Constant image produces no edges canny(constant_image) = zeros Invalid thresholds rejected canny(I, sigma, high, low) returns Err when low > high Canny, 'A Computational Approach to Edge Detection', IEEE TPAMI, 1986"},{"stem":"image-color-v1","path":"contracts/image-color-v1.yaml","description":"Color conversion and connected component labeling","equations":["connected_components","hsv_roundtrip","rgb_to_gray"],"obligation_types":["invariant","invariant","invariant"],"properties":["BT.601 weights","HSV roundtrip","Component connectivity"],"references":["ITU-R BT.601: Studio encoding parameters","Suzuki & Abe, 'Topological structural analysis of digitized binary images', 1985"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":3,"falsification_count":5,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"image-color-v1 Color conversion and connected component labeling connected_components Label(p) = Label(q) ⟺ p and q are 4-connected in foreground All background pixels have label 0 Connected pixels share the same label Disconnected pixels have different labels hsv_roundtrip HSV→RGB→HSV = identity RGB→HSV→RGB = identity within tolerance rgb_to_gray Y = 0.299·R + 0.587·G + 0.114·B White maps to 1.0 Black maps to 0.0 Coefficients sum to 1.0 BT.601 weights gray(1,0,0) = 0.299, gray(0,1,0) = 0.587 HSV roundtrip ||HSV→RGB→HSV(x) - x||_∞ < ε for valid RGB Component connectivity ∀p,q: label(p) = label(q) → 4-path(p,q) ITU-R BT.601: Studio encoding parameters Suzuki & Abe, 'Topological structural analysis of digitized binary images', 1985"},{"stem":"image-conv2d-v1","path":"contracts/image-conv2d-v1.yaml","description":"2D image convolution and edge detection","equations":["conv2d"],"obligation_types":["invariant","equivalence","invariant","invariant"],"properties":["Identity kernel","Separable matches 2D","Constant image preservation under Gaussian","Canny: no edges on uniform image"],"references":["Gonzalez & Woods, 'Digital Image Processing', 4th ed.","Canny, 'A computational approach to edge detection', TPAMI 1986"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":4,"falsification_count":4,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"image-conv2d-v1 2D image convolution and edge detection conv2d O(i,j) = Σ_p Σ_q I(i+p, j+q) · K(p, q) Identity kernel preserves input Separable kernel matches 2D convolution Gaussian blur preserves constant images Identity kernel conv2d(I, δ) = I where δ is the Kronecker delta kernel Separable matches 2D |conv2d(I, v⊗h) - separable(I, v, h)| < ε Constant image preservation under Gaussian |gaussian(c, σ) - c| < ε for constant image c Canny: no edges on uniform image canny(uniform) = empty edge map Gonzalez & Woods, 'Digital Image Processing', 4th ed. Canny, 'A computational approach to edge detection', TPAMI 1986"},{"stem":"image-histogram-v1","path":"contracts/image-histogram-v1.yaml","description":"Histogram, equalization, morphology, resize — NPP parity","equations":["histogram","morphology","resize"],"obligation_types":["invariant","invariant","invariant"],"properties":["Histogram sum equals pixel count","Dilate-erode duality on constant","Resize identity"],"references":["NVIDIA Performance Primitives (NPP) documentation"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":3,"falsification_count":3,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"image-histogram-v1 Histogram, equalization, morphology, resize — NPP parity histogram h[k] = |{(x,y) : floor(I(x,y) * bins) == k}| Sum of histogram bins equals total pixels Each bin count is non-negative morphology dilate(I,S)(x,y) = max{I(x+i,y+j) : S(i,j)=1} Constant image: dilate = erode = original Opening removes small bright features Closing fills small dark features resize R(x,y) = interp(I, x*sx, y*sy) Identity resize preserves image exactly Constant image preserved under any resize Histogram sum equals pixel count Σ h[k] = w * h Dilate-erode duality on constant dilate(C, S) = erode(C, S) = C for constant image C Resize identity resize(I, w, h, w, h) = I NVIDIA Performance Primitives (NPP) documentation"},{"stem":"image-resize-v1","path":"contracts/image-resize-v1.yaml","description":"Image resize with nearest, bilinear, bicubic, and Lanczos interpolation","equations":["resize"],"obligation_types":["invariant","invariant","invariant"],"properties":["Identity resize","Constant preservation","Zero output dimension rejected"],"references":["Keys, 'Cubic Convolution Interpolation for Digital Image Processing', IEEE TASSP, 1981","Turkowski, 'Filters for Common Resampling Tasks', Graphics Gems I, 1990"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":3,"falsification_count":5,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"image-resize-v1 Image resize with nearest, bilinear, bicubic, and Lanczos interpolation resize O(dx,dy) = interp(I, sx(dx), sy(dy)) Same-size resize preserves image Constant image stays constant under any interpolation Output dimensions match requested size Identity resize resize(I, W, H, W, H, method) = I Constant preservation resize(constant_c, W1, H1, W2, H2, method) = constant_c Zero output dimension rejected resize(I, W, H, 0, H2) returns Err Keys, 'Cubic Convolution Interpolation for Digital Image Processing', IEEE TASSP, 1981 Turkowski, 'Filters for Common Resampling Tasks', Graphics Gems I, 1990"},{"stem":"rand-philox-v1","path":"contracts/rand-philox-v1.yaml","description":"Philox 4x32-10 counter-based RNG","equations":["philox"],"obligation_types":["determinism","bound","bound"],"properties":["Reproducibility","Uniform distribution","Normal distribution (Box-Muller)"],"references":["Salmon et al., 'Parallel random numbers: as easy as 1, 2, 3', SC 2011"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":3,"falsification_count":3,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"rand-philox-v1 Philox 4x32-10 counter-based RNG philox output = Philox4x32_10(counter, key) — 10 rounds of Feistel-like bijection Determinism: same (key, counter) → same output Period: 2^128 per key Statistical quality: passes BigCrush Reproducibility Philox(key, ctr) == Philox(key, ctr) for all inputs Uniform distribution chi-squared test on 100-bucket histogram < 150 (p=0.001) Normal distribution (Box-Muller) |mean| < 0.02, |variance - 1.0| < 0.05 for 100k samples Salmon et al., 'Parallel random numbers: as easy as 1, 2, 3', SC 2011"},{"stem":"rand-threefry-v1","path":"contracts/rand-threefry-v1.yaml","description":"Threefry 4x64-20 counter-based PRNG","equations":["threefry"],"obligation_types":["invariant","invariant","bound"],"properties":["Determinism","Different counters produce different output","Uniform distribution"],"references":["Salmon et al., 'Parallel Random Numbers: As Easy as 1, 2, 3', SC 2011"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":3,"falsification_count":5,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"rand-threefry-v1 Threefry 4x64-20 counter-based PRNG threefry output = Threefry_20(counter, key) using rotation + XOR rounds Deterministic: same (key, counter) -> same output No multiplication: uses only add, rotate, XOR Period: 2^256 per key Uniform distribution: passes basic statistical tests Determinism threefry(key, ctr) == threefry(key, ctr) Different counters produce different output threefry(key, c1) != threefry(key, c2) for c1 != c2 (w.h.p.) Uniform distribution |mean(fill_uniform(N)) - 0.5| < 0.01 for N=100000 Salmon et al., 'Parallel Random Numbers: As Easy as 1, 2, 3', SC 2011"},{"stem":"softmax-kernel-v1","path":"contracts/softmax-kernel-v1.yaml","description":"4-pass SIMD softmax with AVX2 max/sum/normalize acceleration","equations":["softmax"],"obligation_types":["equivalence"],"properties":["SIMD and scalar softmax produce identical results"],"references":["GH-384: softmax 0.90x vs ndarray at vocabulary sizes","Milakov & Gimelshein (2018). Online normalizer calculation for softmax","Intel Intrinsics Guide: _mm256_max_ps, _mm256_add_ps, _mm256_mul_ps"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":1,"falsification_count":1,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"softmax-kernel-v1 4-pass SIMD softmax with AVX2 max/sum/normalize acceleration softmax Given x ∈ R^N:\n  m = max_i(x_i)                          # Pass 1: max\n  e_i = exp(x_i - m)   for i = 0..N-1     # Pass 2: exp + store\n  s = Σ_i e_i                              # Pass 3: sum\n  y_i = e_i / s        for i = 0..N-1      # Pass 4: normalize\n SIMD and scalar softmax produce identical results For all valid inputs: softmax_avx2(x) == softmax_scalar(x) GH-384: softmax 0.90x vs ndarray at vocabulary sizes Milakov & Gimelshein (2018). Online normalizer calculation for softmax Intel Intrinsics Guide: _mm256_max_ps, _mm256_add_ps, _mm256_mul_ps"},{"stem":"solve-cholesky-v1","path":"contracts/solve-cholesky-v1.yaml","description":"Cholesky factorization for symmetric positive definite matrices","equations":["cholesky"],"obligation_types":["bound","invariant","invariant"],"properties":["Backward error","Solve residual","Non-SPD rejection"],"references":["Golub & Van Loan, 'Matrix Computations', 4th ed., Chapter 4.2","Higham, 'Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms', 2nd ed."],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":3,"falsification_count":3,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"solve-cholesky-v1 Cholesky factorization for symmetric positive definite matrices cholesky A = L * L^T where L is lower triangular Reconstruction: ||A - L*L^T|| < n * u * ||A|| Solve accuracy: ||Ax - b|| / (||A|| * ||x||) < n * u L is lower triangular with positive diagonal Backward error ||A - L*L^T||_F / ||A||_F <= n * u Solve residual ||Ax - b|| / (||A|| * ||x||) < n * u Non-SPD rejection cholesky(non_spd) returns Err Golub & Van Loan, 'Matrix Computations', 4th ed., Chapter 4.2 Higham, 'Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms', 2nd ed."},{"stem":"solve-lu-v1","path":"contracts/solve-lu-v1.yaml","description":"LU factorization with partial pivoting","equations":["lu_factorization"],"obligation_types":["invariant","invariant","bound"],"properties":["Factorization backward error","Solution residual","Singular matrix detection"],"references":["Golub & Van Loan, 'Matrix Computations', 4th ed., 2013"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":3,"falsification_count":3,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"solve-lu-v1 LU factorization with partial pivoting lu_factorization PA = LU where L unit lower triangular, U upper triangular, P permutation PA = LU within backward error L[i][j] = 0 for j > i, L[i][i] = 1 U[i][j] = 0 for j < i Factorization backward error ||PA - LU||_F / ||A||_F < n · u Solution residual ||Ax - b|| / ||b|| < κ(A) · n · u Singular matrix detection returns Err(SingularMatrix) for rank-deficient A Golub & Van Loan, 'Matrix Computations', 4th ed., 2013"},{"stem":"solve-qr-v1","path":"contracts/solve-qr-v1.yaml","description":"QR factorization via Householder reflections","equations":["qr_factorization"],"obligation_types":["invariant","invariant"],"properties":["Orthogonality","Reconstruction"],"references":["Golub & Van Loan, 'Matrix Computations', 4th ed., 2013"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":2,"falsification_count":2,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"solve-qr-v1 QR factorization via Householder reflections qr_factorization A = QR where Q orthogonal (Q^T Q = I), R upper triangular Q^T Q = I (orthogonality) ||A - QR||_F / ||A||_F < √n · u Orthogonality ||Q^T Q - I||_F < √n · u Reconstruction ||A - QR||_F / ||A||_F < √n · u Golub & Van Loan, 'Matrix Computations', 4th ed., 2013"},{"stem":"solve-svd-v1","path":"contracts/solve-svd-v1.yaml","description":"SVD via one-sided Jacobi rotations","equations":["svd"],"obligation_types":["invariant","ordering","bound","invariant"],"properties":["U orthogonality","Singular values decreasing","Singular values non-negative","Reconstruction"],"references":["Golub & Van Loan, 'Matrix Computations', 4th ed., 2013","Batched QR and SVD on GPUs, arXiv:1707.05141"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":4,"falsification_count":4,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"solve-svd-v1 SVD via one-sided Jacobi rotations svd A = UΣV^T where U,V orthogonal, Σ diagonal non-negative decreasing U^T U = I, V^T V = I σ_i ≥ σ_{i+1} ≥ 0 ||A - UΣV^T||_F / ||A||_F < √min(m,n) · u U orthogonality ||U^T U - I||_F < √m · u Singular values decreasing σ_i ≥ σ_{i+1} for all i Singular values non-negative σ_i ≥ 0 for all i Reconstruction ||A - UΣV^T||_F / ||A||_F < √min(m,n) · u Golub & Van Loan, 'Matrix Computations', 4th ed., 2013 Batched QR and SVD on GPUs, arXiv:1707.05141"},{"stem":"sparse-bsr-v1","path":"contracts/sparse-bsr-v1.yaml","description":"Block Sparse Row (BSR) format — cuSPARSE parity","equations":["bsr_spmv"],"obligation_types":["invariant","invariant"],"properties":["BSR-CSR SpMV equivalence","Alpha-beta scaling"],"references":["cuSPARSE documentation, NVIDIA","Saad, 'Iterative Methods for Sparse Linear Systems', 2003"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":2,"falsification_count":2,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"sparse-bsr-v1 Block Sparse Row (BSR) format — cuSPARSE parity bsr_spmv y = α · A_BSR · x + β · y BSR SpMV matches CSR SpMV for same matrix Identity block matrix: y = α·x + β·y BSR-CSR SpMV equivalence ||y_BSR - y_CSR||_inf < ε Alpha-beta scaling spmv(α,x,β,y) = α·A·x + β·y cuSPARSE documentation, NVIDIA Saad, 'Iterative Methods for Sparse Linear Systems', 2003"},{"stem":"sparse-formats-v1","path":"contracts/sparse-formats-v1.yaml","description":"Sparse matrix format conversions (BSR, SELL)","equations":["sell_spmv"],"obligation_types":["equivalence","invariant"],"properties":["SELL SpMV matches CSR SpMV","Format conversion preserves structure"],"references":["Monakov et al., 'Automatically Tuning Sparse Matrix-Vector Multiplication for GPU Architectures', HiPEAC 2010"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":2,"falsification_count":3,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"sparse-formats-v1 Sparse matrix format conversions (BSR, SELL) sell_spmv y = alpha * A * x + beta * y via SELL (Sliced ELLPACK) format SELL SpMV matches CSR SpMV within ULP tolerance Sliced storage: column-major within each slice Padding: rows within a slice padded to max width SELL SpMV matches CSR SpMV |sell_spmv(A,x) - csr_spmv(A,x)| < epsilon Format conversion preserves structure SellMatrix::from_csr(A).to_dense() == A.to_dense() Monakov et al., 'Automatically Tuning Sparse Matrix-Vector Multiplication for GPU Architectures', HiPEAC 2010"},{"stem":"sparse-spgemm-v1","path":"contracts/sparse-spgemm-v1.yaml","description":"Sparse General Matrix-Matrix Multiply (SpGEMM)","equations":["spgemm"],"obligation_types":["invariant","invariant","invariant"],"properties":["Right identity","Left identity","Known product"],"references":["Gustavson, 'Two fast algorithms for sparse matrices', 1978"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":3,"falsification_count":5,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"sparse-spgemm-v1 Sparse General Matrix-Matrix Multiply (SpGEMM) spgemm C = A * B where A, B, C are sparse CSR Identity: AI = A, IA = A Associativity: (AB)C = A(BC) within tolerance Dimension: C.rows = A.rows, C.cols = B.cols Diagonal: diag(a) * diag(b) = diag(a.*b) Right identity spgemm(A, I) = A Left identity spgemm(I, A) = A Known product spgemm(A, B).to_dense() = dense(A) * dense(B) Gustavson, 'Two fast algorithms for sparse matrices', 1978"},{"stem":"sparse-spmm-v1","path":"contracts/sparse-spmm-v1.yaml","description":"Sparse Matrix-Matrix Multiply (CSR format)","equations":["spmm"],"obligation_types":["equivalence","invariant"],"properties":["Sparse-dense result matches dense-dense reference","Identity matrix: I*B = B"],"references":["Gustavson, 'Two fast algorithms for sparse matrices', 1978"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":2,"falsification_count":2,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"sparse-spmm-v1 Sparse Matrix-Matrix Multiply (CSR format) spmm C = alpha * A * B + beta * C where A is sparse CSR, B,C are dense Output dimensions: C.rows = A.rows, C.cols = B.cols Matches dense reference within backward error Sparse-dense result matches dense-dense reference |spmm(A,B) - dense(A)*B| < epsilon Identity matrix: I*B = B spmm(I, B) = B Gustavson, 'Two fast algorithms for sparse matrices', 1978"},{"stem":"sparse-spmv-v1","path":"contracts/sparse-spmv-v1.yaml","description":"Sparse Matrix-Vector Multiply (CSR format)","equations":["spmv"],"obligation_types":["invariant","equivalence","bound","linearity"],"properties":["Output dimension correctness","Sparse result matches dense reference","Backward error bound (LAProof)","SpMV is linear in x"],"references":["Merrill & Garland, 'Merge-Based Parallel Sparse Matrix-Vector Multiplication', PPoPP 2016","LAProof: Princeton formal verification of SpMV in C (Appel et al.)"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":4,"falsification_count":4,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"sparse-spmv-v1 Sparse Matrix-Vector Multiply (CSR format) spmv y_i = alpha * sum_j(A_ij * x_j) + beta * y_i Output length equals number of rows: len(y) == m Backward error bounded: |Ay - y_exact| <= nnz_per_row * u * |A| * |x| SpMV is linear in x Output dimension correctness len(result) == A.rows() Sparse result matches dense reference |sparse_spmv(A,x) - dense_matvec(A.to_dense(), x)| < epsilon Backward error bound (LAProof) ||Ax_computed - Ax_exact||_inf <= n * u * ||A||_inf * ||x||_inf SpMV is linear in x |spmv(A, alpha*x + beta*y) - alpha*spmv(A,x) - beta*spmv(A,y)| < epsilon Merrill & Garland, 'Merge-Based Parallel Sparse Matrix-Vector Multiplication', PPoPP 2016 LAProof: Princeton formal verification of SpMV in C (Appel et al.)"},{"stem":"tensor-contraction-v1","path":"contracts/tensor-contraction-v1.yaml","description":"Einstein summation via TTGT — tensor contraction correctness","equations":["einsum"],"obligation_types":["invariant","invariant","invariant","bound","bound"],"properties":["Matmul correctness","Matmul associativity","Transpose involution","Dimension mismatch detection","Invalid subscript detection"],"references":["Springer et al., 'TTGT: A General Approach for Tensor Contractions on GPUs', 2017","cuTENSOR documentation, NVIDIA"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":5,"falsification_count":5,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"tensor-contraction-v1 Einstein summation via TTGT — tensor contraction correctness einsum C_{out} = Σ_{contracted} A_{a_labels} · B_{b_labels} Output shape matches output label dimensions Contracted dimensions must match between inputs Matrix multiply is associative: (AB)C = A(BC) Transpose is involutory: (A^T)^T = A Matmul correctness einsum('ij,jk->ik', A, B) = standard matrix product Matmul associativity ||einsum(AB,C) - einsum(A,BC)||_F < ε Transpose involution transpose(transpose(A, p), p^{-1}) = A Dimension mismatch detection returns Err when contracted indices have different sizes Invalid subscript detection returns Err for malformed subscript strings Springer et al., 'TTGT: A General Approach for Tensor Contractions on GPUs', 2017 cuTENSOR documentation, NVIDIA"},{"stem":"transpose-kernel-v1","path":"contracts/transpose-kernel-v1.yaml","description":"AVX2 8×8 in-register transpose with two-level cache blocking","equations":["transpose"],"obligation_types":["equivalence"],"properties":["SIMD and scalar transpose produce identical results"],"references":["GH-388: Transpose 242x slower than ndarray at attention shapes","Intel 64 and IA-32 Architectures Optimization Reference Manual, §11.12","Lam, Rothberg & Wolf (1991). Cache Performance of Blocked Algorithms. ASPLOS"],"depends_on":[],"is_registry":false,"obligation_count":1,"falsification_count":1,"kani_count":1,"corpus_text":"transpose-kernel-v1 AVX2 8×8 in-register transpose with two-level cache blocking transpose B[j * rows + i] = A[i * cols + j] SIMD and scalar transpose produce identical results For all valid inputs: transpose_avx2(A) == transpose_scalar(A) GH-388: Transpose 242x slower than ndarray at attention shapes Intel 64 and IA-32 Architectures Optimization Reference Manual, §11.12 Lam, Rothberg & Wolf (1991). Cache Performance of Blocked Algorithms. ASPLOS"}],"score_cache":{"fft-bluestein-v1":0.465,"image-histogram-v1":0.465,"gemv-kernel-v1":0.585,"rand-threefry-v1":0.465,"solve-svd-v1":0.44625000000000004,"sparse-bsr-v1":0.5025000000000001,"image-color-v1":0.465,"solve-qr-v1":0.5025000000000001,"blas-trsm-v1":0.5025000000000001,"tensor-contraction-v1":0.435,"transpose-kernel-v1":0.585,"image-canny-v1":0.465,"sparse-spgemm-v1":0.465,"rand-philox-v1":0.465,"image-resize-v1":0.48500000000000004,"softmax-kerne